Since you've mastered the this keyword, let's build a Tea object. Why? Because making tea is a perfect analogy for building an object step-by-step (water, sugar, milk, ginger).
If we used a standard constructor, we might get confused: new Tea(true, false, true, true). What does the first true mean? Sugar? Ginger?
The Builder Pattern makes it clear.
1. The Complete Code (The "Tea" Builder)
Paste this into your IDE and read the comments. They explain the "magic" of each part.
public class Tea {
    // These are final because once the tea is made, you can't change it (Immutability)
    private final boolean hasSugar;
    private final boolean hasMilk;
    private final boolean hasGinger;

    // Private constructor: No one can call 'new Tea()' directly!
    // They MUST use the Builder.
    private Tea(Builder builder) {
        this.hasSugar = builder.hasSugar;
        this.hasMilk = builder.hasMilk;
        this.hasGinger = builder.hasGinger;
    }

    // This is the Static Inner Class (The "Waiter" taking your order)
    public static class Builder {
        private boolean hasSugar;
        private boolean hasMilk;
        private boolean hasGinger;

        // Method for Sugar
        public Builder addSugar() {
            this.hasSugar = true;
            return this; // Returns the "Waiter" so you can add more things
        }

        // Method for Milk
        public Builder addMilk() {
            this.hasMilk = true;
            return this;
        }

        // Method for Ginger
        public Builder addGinger() {
            this.hasGinger = true;
            return this;
        }

        // The Final Step: Create the actual Tea object
        public Tea build() {
            return new Tea(this); 
        }
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "Tea [Sugar=" + hasSugar + ", Milk=" + hasMilk + ", Ginger=" + hasGinger + "]";
    }
}

2. How to use it (The "Method Chaining")
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        
        // This is the beauty of the Builder Pattern:
        Tea mySpecialTea = new Tea.Builder()
                                .addSugar()
                                .addGinger()
                                .build();

        System.out.println(mySpecialTea);
    }
}

3. Step-by-Step Explanation
Step A: The "Private" Vault
The Tea constructor is private. This is a senior move. It prevents anyone from creating an "incomplete" tea. You are forcing the developer to use the Builder process.
Step B: The "this" Hand-off
When you call .addSugar(), the builder sets its internal hasSugar to true and says return this.
 * this is the Builder object.
 * Because it returns itself, you can immediately hit . again to call .addGinger().
Step C: The "Build" Moment
The .build() method is where the real Tea object is born.
 * It takes all the values the Builder collected and passes them to the private Tea constructor.
 * It's like the Waiter finally taking your order to the kitchen.
💡 Why this is great for your TCS projects:
 * Readable: addSugar() is much easier to read than true, false, true.
 * Safe: If you forget to add milk, the code won't crash; it just builds a tea without milk.
 * Immutable: Once build() is called, the Tea object cannot be changed. This is very important for Thread Safety in Java.
